  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Karehanumappa vs K.S.R.T.C on 6 February, 2025 Kabc020016782024 Before Motor Vehicles Accident Claims Tribunal, Bengaluru City Scch-17 Present: Sri. Kanchi Mayanna Goutam B.A.L, Ll.M., Member, Mact Xix Addl. Judge, Court of Small Causes, Bengaluru. Dated this the 6th day of February- 2025 Mvc No.346/2024 Petitioner/S: Sri. Karehanumappa, S/o. Gujjappa,",
    "facts": "The petitioner has filed this petition U/Sec.166 of the Motor Vehicles Act claiming compensation for the injuries sustained by him in a road traffic accident that occurred on 10.4.2023. 2. The petition averments in brief are as under: On 10.4.2023 at about 12.20 p.m., the petitioner was traveling as a passenger in a KSRTC bus bearing No. KA-06-F-1183 in right side seat 4th window from back side Karehanumappa vs K.S.R.T.C on 6 February, 2025 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/145774666/ 1 and when the said bus reached near Nallekamanahalli village, the driver of the said bus drove the same in a rash and negligent manner, as a result, the bus right side tyre bursted and the aluminium sheet hit the petitioner's right leg and both bones of the right leg fractured. Immediately after the accident, he was shifted to Govt. Hospital, Midigeshi and Madhugiri and after first aid he was shifted to District Hospital, Tumkur and later he was shifted to Hemavathi Orthopedic and Trauma Centre, Tumkur wherein he was admitted as inpatient and he has spent Rs.2,00,000/- towards hospitalization charges, treatment medicines, conveyance, nourishing food and other incidental charges etc., Prior to the accident, petitioner was very hale and healthy and he was working as agriculturist and also doing flower business and earning Rs.25,000/- p.m. Due to the accidental injuries petitioner is not able to continue his work and is under complete bed rest and he is suffering from permanent disability. The respondent is the owner and internal insurer of the bus is liable to pay compensation of Rs.25,00,000/- with interest. 3. After service of notices respondent appeared through its counsel and filed written statement by denying that the accident was occurred due to negligence on the part of driver of KSRTC bus bearing No. KA-06-F- 1183. Further denied the age, avocation, alleged disability and income of the petitioner. The compensation claimed by the petitioner is highly excessive and exorbitant. Hence, the respondent prays to dismiss the petition against it. In order to prove the claim petition, the petitioner examined himself as P.W.1 and got marked the documents at Ex.P.1 to 10. Also examined Dr. Nagaraj B.N. Orthopaedic Surgeon as PW.2 and got marked Ex.P11 & 12. In spite of sufficient opportunity, the respondent has not chosen to lead evidence.",
    "arguments": "At this juncture, I would like to quote the following judgment wherein it states that; 2009 ACJ 287 ( National Ins. Co. V/s. Pushparama and others), wherein it is held that, Certified copy of the criminal court records such as FIR, recovery and mechanical inspection of the vehicle or documents are of sufficient proof to reach the conclusion that the rider was negligent. In a claim for compensation under Section 166 of Motor Vehicles Act, 1988, the claimant is to prove the incident only on preponderance of probabilities and the standard of proof beyond reasonable doubt is not required as held by Hon'ble Supreme Court in the decision reported in 2011 SAR (CIVIL) 319 Kusum and others V/s Satbir and others. 16. Further the Hon'ble Supreme Court in case of Bimla Devi and others v. Himachal Road Transport Corporation and others (2009) 13 SCC 530, wherein it is held that, it was necessary to be borne in mind that strict proof of an accident caused by a particular bus in a particular manner may not be possible to be done by the claimants.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "At this juncture, I would like to quote the following judgment wherein it states that; 2009 ACJ 287 ( National Ins. Co. V/s. Pushparama and others), wherein it is held that, Certified copy of the criminal court records such as FIR, recovery and mechanical inspection of the vehicle or documents are of sufficient proof to reach the conclusion that the rider was negligent. In a claim for compensation under Section 166 of Motor Vehicles Act, 1988, the claimant is to prove the incident only on preponderance of probabilities and the standard of proof beyond reasonable doubt is not required as held by Hon'ble Supreme Court in the decision reported in 2011 SAR (CIVIL) 319 Kusum and others V/s Satbir and others. 16. Further the Hon'ble Supreme Court in case of Bimla Devi and others v. Himachal Road Transport Corporation and others (2009) 13 SCC 530, wherein it is held that, it was necessary to be borne in mind that strict proof of an accident caused by a particular bus in a particular manner may not be possible to be done by the claimants."
  },
